an estimated one in 20 people who  suffered from the disease are so-called  covet long-haulers that means they  recovered from their initial illness but  often have debilitating symptoms for  weeks or even months  many can't go back to work and face  severe financial hurdles  as part of our series medical price  roulette in collaboration with clear  health costs  our consumer investigative correspondent  anna warner looks at some of the  challenges they face  anna good morning to you yeah good  morning the long covered patients we  spoke to  say they're sometimes so disabled by  symptoms they can't get out of bed  but if they can't go back to work how do  they survive financially  it is day 154 from me  and i am considered a covert long hauler  that was candace taylor of atlanta last  august  struggling to speak her covid19  infection happened in march 2020  but she says she still has breathing  issues and other symptoms  i am pretty much disabled i can't  do small tests that we take for granted  i can't clean  my house without severe chest pain  shooting throughout my body covid hit  kelly keaney  of new jersey over a year ago too now  she says  the fatigue and pain have turned her  into a different person  i don't look like that anymore i do not  look healthy  i and i don't feel healthy  emotionally or physically malaina  barcenas is an industrial janitor in  detroit  i get tired very easily still hi  barcenas gave birth to her daughter  about 16 months ago while suffering from  covid  and in a coma the walking is the biggest  issue  going from the door to the car that can  tire me out like for the whole day  doctors diagnosed all three women with  what's often called  long covet we're dealing with a syndrome  that we know is quite devastating dr avi  natt is studying  long-covered patients at the national  institutes of health or nih  how disabling can this be it can be  very severe many people cannot work any  longer so they lost their job  and now they are unemployed keaney knows  about that  when dogs have a job to do they're not  bored  and barking she's lost both her jobs her  personal dog training business  and the full-time mortgage processing  job that paid the bills  she says she tried to return to that job  until the day she says her boss told her  she was making too many mistakes  you've been with me 27 years you don't  make the same mistake and you certainly  don't make them twice  you're not remembering what i tell you  to do she's been out on disability with  her boss's support for almost 10 months  but no one can say how long that's going  to last  do you have any assurance right now that  you are going to be able to go back to  work  no barcenas is on disability too  but says her payments are roughly half  her usual salary and she lost her health  insurance in september  two weeks after that i got a bill for  physical therapy for almost three  thousand dollars  it was like and no real idea of how to  pay it right  exactly they're the kind of stories  connecticut employment law attorney gary  phelan hears a lot  i think they're people facing the most  enormous challenge of their lives  phelan says many of his clients with  long covid can't work  but have trouble even getting disability  payments from the insurance plans  provided by their employers  candace taylor for example it has been  a tug of war with the insurance company  she worked at a hospital that provides  disability insurance  but even though her doctor diagnosed her  with postcovid syndrome  the insurance company fought her claim  in march they sent this letter denying  short-term benefits even claiming in  part falsely taylor says  that she had a psychosomatic illness in  other words one  all in her mind i never thought i would  have to suffer for  benefits that i worked for are a lot of  insurance companies turning down  people's disability claims i think  generally that has been the case  a lot of them are based on you know  symptoms that it's hard to define  they change over time unlike the nature  of other medical conditions  and so because of that uncertainty it  gives  really insurance companies uh more you  know  more of an ability to deny claims at the  time of our interview  taylor said she was three months behind  on her rent  and had gotten two eviction notices  there's days where i'm sad there's days  where  i want to give up but i know i can't  give up  there's a small part of me that just has  to keep fighting  kelly keaney tries to keep her spirits  up too but says what really hurts most  is losing the dog training business she  loved  that she hoped would eventually become  her full-time livelihood  and i don't know if i'm going to be able  to reopen my business  it breaks my heart really does  well taylor fought her insurance company  new york life for nearly a year  the company wouldn't discuss her case  with us citing privacy and  confidentiality concerns  but just two days after we contacted  them she was approved  for both short and long-term disability  well that's good news i mean they can  imagine the slap in the face when you're  paying a company to protect you and then  they say oh your illness is all in your  head that's quite the insult  uh what is the main stumbling block for  these people trying to pursue their  disability claims  well i think part of the problem is this  syndrome is so new everybody's still  learning about it and so employers and  probably to some degree the insurance  companies  are still catching up with this the  other part as the attorney pointed out  is  the symptoms can change from day to day  you know there's so many  it's a variety of symptoms and so an  insurance company might say well you  know what we just don't  see where the consistent diagnosis is  here so we're not going to approve your  claim  are legislators willing to step in here  yeah so the nih  has they've congress gave the nih more  than a billion dollars  for research and possible treatments and  the nih  and cdc doctors also say another key  here  is educating doctors treating physicians  to how this plays out what are the  symptoms what do you look for yeah  it's so scary because there's so much  that's unknown i mean you think you get  it  and you survive and yet you still have  these problems i really do hope that  they all just hang on i felt from this  taylor and yeah  i really did and you think if you have a  letter from your doctor then you're  going to be okay with your insurance  company but clearly that's not always  the case  no and and you have to a lot of times  one of the pieces of advice here is if  you're suffering through this  try to get yourself a lawyer if you can  somebody who deals with employment law  because this stuff is  tricky yeah that's not what you want to  hear when you're feeling like now  you need to get a lawyer all right anna  thank you very much
 

